WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.869 --> 00:00:09.675
Most people are familiar with the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World―astounding works of art, architecture, and design

2
00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:12.577
that have inspired awe and admiration for thousands of years.

3
00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:18.761
They include the Colossus of Rhodes, the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, and the Lighthouse at Alexandria.

4
00:00:19.502 --> 00:00:23.485
Today only one of the ancient wonders remains standing: the Great Pyramids of Giza.

5
00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:30.862
In 1994 the American Society of Civil Engineers wanted to pay tribute to the greatest engineering accomplishments of

6
00:00:30.902 --> 00:00:31.603
the modern world.

7
00:00:32.443 --> 00:00:37.807
After consulting engineers the world over, the society decided on a list of Seven Wonders of the Modern

8
00:00:37.847 --> 00:00:45.492
World. This new list was chosen to demonstrate modern society's ability to achieve unachievable feats and reach unreachable

9
00:00:45.532 --> 00:00:52.237
heights. It includes the Empire State Building, the Golden Gate Bridge, the Channel Tunnel, the CN Tower, the

10
00:00:52.277 --> 00:00:56.420
Panama Canal, the Itaipu Dam, and the Delta Works in the North Sea.

11
00:00:57.388 --> 00:01:02.009
The Empire State Building is a 102-story skyscraper in downtown New York.

12
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:08.871
When it was completed in 1931, the Empire State Building became the benchmark for all other skyscrapers that

13
00:01:08.931 --> 00:01:12.972
followed because it combined beautiful architecture with solid engineering.

14
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:18.934
For over fifty years, it was the tallest building in the world, and it remains the tallest building

15
00:01:18.974 --> 00:01:19.474
in New York.

16
00:01:20.294 --> 00:01:25.636
In its early days, when many of its offices were vacant, its nickname was the Empty State Building.

17
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.803
Today it is New York's most visited tourist attraction and an unmistakable part of the city's skyline.

18
00:01:32.904 --> 00:01:37.307
In Toronto, Canada, the CN Tower soars over half a kilometer into the sky.

19
00:01:38.007 --> 00:01:42.450
It is twice as high as the Eiffel Tower and is the tallest free-standing structure on land.

20
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:49.714
It was built in the 1970s when Toronto's radio and TV stations were having difficulty transmitting their broadcasts,

21
00:01:49.754 --> 00:01:53.176
due to the fact that many new skyscrapers were blocking the signals.

22
00:01:53.996 --> 00:01:58.718
The transmitters at the peak of the CN Tower now provide residents of Toronto with some of the

23
00:01:58.758 --> 00:02:01.359
best radio and television reception in North America.

24
00:02:02.199 --> 00:02:08.102
It may originally have been built for practical reasons, but since it opened in 1976, the CN Tower

25
00:02:08.162 --> 00:02:11.203
has become one of Canada's most popular tourist attractions.

26
00:02:12.023 --> 00:02:16.945
Visitors ascend the tower in a glass elevator to an observation deck and a revolving restaurant.

27
00:02:17.686 --> 00:02:21.687
When the weather is clear, they can see across Lake Ontario into New York State.

28
00:02:22.388 --> 00:02:23.048
It's quite a view!

29
00:02:24.064 --> 00:02:28.788
Plans for a tunnel linking England and France were first proposed almost two hundred years ago.

30
00:02:29.469 --> 00:02:34.073
But it was not until 1994 that engineers succeeded in building the Channel Tunnel.

31
00:02:34.873 --> 00:02:40.218
For centuries travel between France and England, which are only 34 kilometers apart, was arduous.

32
00:02:40.898 --> 00:02:44.621
The fastest crossing took seven hours; in bad weather it could take weeks.

33
00:02:45.222 --> 00:02:49.265
Today, thanks to the Channel Tunnel, the journey takes only twenty minutes by train.

34
00:02:50.006 --> 00:02:52.348
The construction of the tunnel, however, was not easy.

35
00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:58.455
It took over fifteen thousand workers seven years to build, tunneling from both sides and meeting in the

36
00:02:58.495 --> 00:03:04.477
middle. Not since the Ice Age, which was thirteen thousand years ago, could people walk between the two

37
00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:07.859
countries. The Channel Tunnel is truly a modern wonder.

38
00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:14.242
The Golden Gate Bridge in San Francisco, which opened in 1937, also links two pieces of land.

39
00:03:15.142 --> 00:03:19.124
It spans 1.28 kilometers, making it one of the longest bridges in the world.

40
00:03:19.956 --> 00:03:25.519
Because San Francisco sits next to the Pacific Ocean and the mouth of a harbor, the city frequently

41
00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:27.100
experiences dense fog.

42
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.683
So architects chose to paint the bridge a bright orange color for greater visibility.

43
00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:38.206
This orange bridge has become a symbol for the San Francisco area, as well as a famous icon

44
00:03:38.246 --> 00:03:39.366
of advanced engineering.

45
00:03:40.147 --> 00:03:44.989
Over the years the Golden Gate Bridge has survived strong winds, ocean currents, and even earthquakes.

46
00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.904
Experts never thought that it would last so long in such a turbulent environment, but the bridge has

47
00:03:51.004 --> 00:03:54.686
only been closed three times in its entire history due to inclement weather.

48
00:03:55.506 --> 00:03:59.988
Even the strong earthquake that hit the area in 1989 did not damage the Golden Gate Bridge.

49
00:04:00.949 --> 00:04:06.611
Another wonder that crosses water, the Itaipu Dam, is located deep in the forests of South America on

50
00:04:06.651 --> 00:04:08.612
the border between Brazil and Paraguay.

51
00:04:09.452 --> 00:04:13.954
It was built in 1982 to supply water to drought-stricken areas of both countries.

52
00:04:14.914 --> 00:04:21.333
Its construction involved redirecting the seventh largest river in the world, the Parana River, and moving over fifty

53
00:04:21.393 --> 00:04:22.853
million tons of dirt and rock.

54
00:04:23.753 --> 00:04:26.294
This massive dam is nearly eight kilometers long.

55
00:04:27.254 --> 00:04:31.155
All in all, it required fifteen times more concrete than the Channel Tunnel.

56
00:04:31.916 --> 00:04:35.997
It was selected as a Wonder of the Modern World not just because of the effort it took

57
00:04:36.057 --> 00:04:40.778
to build, but also because it houses the largest hydroelectric power plant in the world.

58
00:04:41.678 --> 00:04:46.340
The Delta Works in the Netherlands, the least-known Wonder of the Modern World, are an amazing feat of

59
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:53.203
engineering. In 1953 high sea levels in the North Sea flooded much of the low-lying country, crashing through

60
00:04:53.243 --> 00:04:54.303
dikes and seawalls.

61
00:04:55.084 --> 00:04:58.705
Almost two thousand people were killed and seventy thousand were evacuated.

62
00:04:59.506 --> 00:05:05.649
Engineers immediately began plans to dam four major rivers and tidal inlets to prevent a similar event from

63
00:05:05.709 --> 00:05:06.389
happening again.

64
00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:11.432
Twice a day water flows in and out through these inlets, where the rivers widen to meet the

65
00:05:11.492 --> 00:05:17.647
sea. In order to prevent further flooding, engineers had to build a system of locks, channels, and gates

66
00:05:17.747 --> 00:05:22.550
to control the flow of this water so that it would always be directed out to sea, not

67
00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:23.551
onto neighboring land.

68
00:05:24.371 --> 00:05:29.434
This was a difficult process that began in 1958 and took more than thirty years to complete.

69
00:05:30.214 --> 00:05:34.917
Today the Delta Works ensures that the people of the Netherlands are protected from rising sea levels.

70
00:05:35.878 --> 00:05:39.320
The Panama Canal has the longest history of all the Wonders.

71
00:05:40.084 --> 00:05:46.329
Nearly five hundred years ago in 1534, Charles I of Spain proposed the construction of a canal that

72
00:05:46.349 --> 00:05:51.392
would link the Atlantic Ocean north of Colon, Panama, to the Pacific Ocean, south of Panama City.

73
00:05:52.173 --> 00:05:58.217
Work on the canal didn't actually start until centuries later; it began in 1881 and was completed in

74
00:05:58.277 --> 00:06:04.742
1914. The Panama Canal has a series of locks to compensate for a difference of 5.9 meters between

75
00:06:04.782 --> 00:06:07.104
the levels of the Pacific and Atlantic oceans.

76
00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:13.122
When a ship travels through from the Pacific Ocean, it enters the first of many sequential locks.

77
00:06:13.962 --> 00:06:17.463
Each one is flooded to gradually raise the water level higher and higher.

78
00:06:18.103 --> 00:06:22.124
When the lock water level reaches the same height as the water on the other side, the ship

79
00:06:22.204 --> 00:06:24.645
is released and it may sail out into the next lock.

80
00:06:25.325 --> 00:06:30.307
It takes ships about nine hours to journey through all of the locks, finally reaching the Atlantic Ocean.

81
00:06:31.007 --> 00:06:33.888
Approximately twelve thousand ships travel through it each year.

82
00:06:34.884 --> 00:06:40.526
As the members of the American Society of Civil Engineers said, "The original wonders were amazing sites to

83
00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:44.147
behold, but today's modern wonders are more than simply awe inspiring.

84
00:06:44.787 --> 00:06:49.968
They are operational masterpieces that have revolutionized civil engineering and benefited humanity."

85
00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:56.290
But surely there are other superb examples of engineering and design that have not been included in this

86
00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:02.152
list. Don't worryᅳanother list called the Forgotten Modern Wonders of the World has been compiled.

87
00:07:03.016 --> 00:07:07.999
It includes famous landmarks, such as the Eiffel Tower in Paris; the clock in London known as Big

88
00:07:08.039 --> 00:07:13.362
Ben; the Petronas Towers in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia; and the Aswan High Dam in Egypt.

89
00:07:14.042 --> 00:07:17.584
The next time you take a trip, try to visit one of the Wonders of the Modern World

90
00:07:18.044 --> 00:07:20.085
and be inspired by human ingenuity.
